{"content": "The regression is exactly the `__getattr__` dunder-callable bug described in CHANGELOG (issue672). Before the fix, accessing any dunder attribute (e.g. `__deepcopy__`, `__reduce__` used internally by `copy.deepcopy`/`copy.copy`) on a `_TokenType`/`Token` object returned a new `_TokenType` instance instead of raising `AttributeError`. When code like `copy.deepcopy()` then tried to call that returned attribute as `obj.__deepcopy__(memo)`, it failed with `TypeError: '_TokenType' object is not callable` since `_TokenType` instances aren't callable.\n\nI already applied the fix in `sqlparse/tokens.py`'s `_TokenType.__getattr__`: it now raises `AttributeError` for any name that looks like a dunder (`__foo__`), restoring normal Python attribute-lookup semantics (so `copy`/`pickle`/etc. correctly fall through to default behavior) while still allowing all other attribute access (e.g. `Token.Keyword`, `Token.DML`) to work as before, creating/caching child token types.\n\nLet me verify the current file state, then run a quick sanity check reproducing the issue (copy.deepcopy on a Token/parsed statement) and run the existing test suite if present to make sure nothing else broke.", "tool_calls": [
